 note we give a counterexample to this conjecture.\n\n## Keywords\n\nGraph coloring\n\n;\n\nColoring complex\n\n;\n\nGraph homomorphism--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOAI Math · Prove2Me (https://prove2.me/collections/oai-math?offset=80)\nciteturn4search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Clow, Haxell and Mohar disprove Lovász's stronger deletion conjecture at $r=3$r=3 (doi:10.1007/s00493-026-00220-3); those examples do not contradict Ryser's bound.\n  * 1983. Tuza proves the intersecting case for $r=5$r=5; Gyárfás had handled $r\\le4$r≤4 (historical account in Király–Tóthmérész, doi:10.37236/6448).\n  * 1991. Erdős, Gyárfás and Pyber record the equivalence with the monochromatic tree-cover formulation (doi:10.1016/0095-8956(91)90007-7).\n  * 2001. Aharoni proves the full case $r=3$r=3 (doi:10.1007/s004930170001).\n  * 2012. Haxell and Scott prove $\\tau\\le(r-\\varepsilon_r)\\nu$τ≤(r−ε r​)ν for $r=4,5$r=4,5 (doi:10.37236/1175).\n  * 2017. Francetić, Herke, McKay and Wanless verify the linear intersecting case through rank nine (doi:10.1016/j.ejc.2016.10.004); Haxell and Scott construct intersecting examples with $\\tau\\ge r-4$τ≥r−4 (doi:10.37236/6460).\n  * 2019. Abu-Khazneh, Barát, Pokrovskiy and Szabó construct intersecting $(q+2)$(q+2)-partite hypergraphs with $\\tau=q+1$τ=q+1, matching the conjectured bound, for prime powers $q$q (doi:10.1016/j.jcta.2018.07.011).\n  * 2025–2026. Clow, Haxell and Mohar disprove Lovász's stronger deletion conjecture at $r=3$r=3 (doi:10.1007/s00493-026-00220-3); those examples do not contradict Ryser's bound.\n\nThe source of this mission, an OpenAI preprint dated September 27, 2026, claims counterexamples to the conjecture itself, already in the intersecting case and with all parts of equal size.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLászló Lovász | ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/author/7004194778/laszlo-lovasz)\nciteturn4search11 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month;     * View PDF ... Our main result is a counterexample to the following conjecture of Lovász, which is often referred to as saying that “every extremal graph theory problem has a finitely forcible optimum”: every finite feasible set of subgraph density constraints can be extended further by a finite set of density constraints such that the resulting set is satisfied by an asymptotically unique graph. ... The main theorem of this paper provides partial results on some major open problems in graph theory, such as Tutteʼs 3-flow conjecture (from the 1970s) that every 4-edge connected graph admits a nowhere-zero 3-flow, the conjecture of Jaeger, Linial, Payan and Tarsi (1992) that every 5-edge-connected graph is -connected, Jaegerʼs circular flow conjecture (1984) that for every odd natural number , every -edge-connected graph has a modulo k-orientation, etc.\nExtremal graph theory and finite forcibility\n\nA. Grzesik, … L.M. Lovász\n\nElectronic Notes in Discrete Mathematics • Volume 61 • August 2017\n\n    * View abstract\n\nWe study the uniqueness of optimal solutions to extremal graph theory problems. Our main result is a counterexample to the following conjecture of Lovász, which is often referred to as saying that “every extremal graph theory problem has a finitely forcible optimum”: every finite feasible set of subgraph density constraints can be extended further by a finite set of density constraints such that the resulting set is satisfied by an asymptotically unique graph.\n\n  * Research article Open archive\n\nOn derivatives of graphon parameters\n\nL.M. Lovász, Y. Zhao\n\nJournal of Combinatorial Theory. Series A • Volume 145 • January 2017\n\n    * View PDF\n    * View abstract\n\nWe give a short elementary proof of the main theorem in the paper “Differential calculus on graphon space” by Diao et al. (2015) , which says that any graphon parameters whose -th derivatives all vanish must be a linear combination of homomorphism densities over graphs H on at most N edges.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA counterexample to a conjecture of Björner and Lovász on the $χ$-coloring complex (https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0405339)\nciteturn4academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 22.5 years ago; Title: A counterexample to a conjecture of Björner and Lovász on the $χ$-coloring complex\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nList of unsolved problems in mathematics (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_unsolved_problems_in_mathematics)\nciteturn4search16 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; (See also Hales-Jewett theorem and n^{d} game) ... An instance of the Erdős-Faber-Lovász conjecture: a graph formed from four cliques of four vertices each, any two of which intersect in a single vertex, can be four-colored. ... *  Akiyama, Jin; Exoo, Geoffrey; Harary, Frank (1981). ... Some unsolved problems in number theory (PDF). ... \"A Counterexample to the Mizohata-Takeuchi Conjecture\". arXiv:2502.06137 [math.CA]. ... *  \"Antoine Song | Clay Mathematics Institute\". ... ... *  Dujmović, Vida; Eppstein, David; Hickingbotham, Robert; Morin, Pat; Wood, David R. ... \"Further results on tree labellings\".\n"
